# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — never commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Pseudonymization mapping — links real PSC/registry persons to their synthetic
# replacements. Committing it defeats the pseudonymization entirely.
**/pseudonym-map.*
**/*-pseudonym-map.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fetched public source data — large, re-fetchable, sometimes licence-bound.
# Commit the fetch manifest (source, URL, retrieval date, checksum), not payloads.
# GLEIF LEI-CDF alone is ~GB scale; a single ABS-EE file is tens of MB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
sources/**
!sources/*/
!sources/**/manifest.json
!sources/**/manifest.yaml
!sources/.gitkeep
!sources/README.md

# Screening output is a decision artifact, not a fetched payload: it records
# which obligors were selected and which §F conditions each satisfies. It must
# survive a restart and be reviewable in diff, so it is tracked despite living
# under sources/. Small (~200KB) and derived from public metadata only.
!sources/screening/
!sources/screening/*.json

# Wave 14 (abs-securitization-surveillance) commits four small JSON artifacts
# describing what was loaded: the deal registry, the prospectus extract, the
# engineered trigger amendment, and the internal overlay. The PAYLOADS beside
# them -- ~5.4 GB of ABS-EE XML and filing HTML -- stay ignored by the
# `sources/**` rule above. Only `sources/absee/*.json` is un-ignored, and the
# payloads live one directory deeper, so this cannot catch them.
!sources/absee/*.json

# GLEIF Level 1 for the 103 non-anchor family-tree entities. The three raw API
# pages (288KB) stay ignored; the parsed 52KB record is the input to
# `loaders/load_gleif_subsidiaries.py`, so a fresh checkout can load without
# re-fetching. Same reasoning as sources/screening/ above: small, derived,
# public metadata only.
!sources/gleif_l1/subsidiaries_l1.json

# Wave 17 (aml-typology-match) Task 1's advisory selection is a reviewed
# DECISION artifact, not a fetched payload: it records which four FinCEN
# advisories anchor the typology claims and why. The cached advisory-page
# HTML fetched alongside it (to resolve each pdf_url) stays ignored under the
# `sources/**` rule above; only the decision and its manifest are tracked.
!sources/fincen/selection.json

# Wave 18 Task 12's SNC report extract is the derived text + retrieval
# metadata `loaders/load_mongo_wave18.py` reads to build the `document`
# row -- small (~30KB), derived only, no privacy concern. The fetched PDF
# payload beside it stays ignored under the `sources/**` rule above.
!sources/snc/extract.json

# The USASpending recipient allow-list is a HAND-VERIFIED DECISION artifact, not
# fetched output. USASpending carries no LEI and no CIK, so every row in it is a
# human judgement about whether a federal award recipient is really our obligor.
# Without it committed, `generators/fetch_usaspending.py` exits 1 on a fresh
# checkout and the curation has to be redone from scratch. Small, and the whole
# point of the source.
!sources/usaspending/
!sources/usaspending/recipient_allowlist.json

# ...except the passage corpus, which is a large regenerable intermediate
# rather than a decision artifact. It embeds full document text (49MB) and the
# authoritative copy now lives in MongoDB fibo_v2. Regenerate with
# generators/segment_passages.py.
sources/screening/passages.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated / regenerable build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build/
out/
generated/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
# SQLite WAL sidecars. `*.sqlite` alone does not cover these, so any run
# that opens the departmental store in WAL mode leaves two untracked
# files behind and `git add -A` commits half a database -- which is the
# thing this file's own "never commit a database" rule is about.
*.sqlite-shm
*.sqlite-wal

# Loader intermediates (regenerated from generators/).
# docs/fibo_files/ is exempt — those are the historical v1 artifacts the
# backstory document refers to, and are kept as reference.
**/mongo-entities.json
**/mongo-relationships.json
*.bak
!docs/fibo_files/**

# Embeddings — large, regenerable, model-version-dependent
**/embeddings/
*.npy
*.faiss

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.egg-info/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor / OS noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
._*
.vscode/
.idea/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude Code per-machine state (project settings stay committed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/settings.local.json

# Wave 13a: cached EDGAR narrative filings. Large, re-fetchable, and the
# derived sections live in sources/screening/wave13a_narrative_index.json.
sources/narrative/*.htm
sources/narrative/*.html

# Ledger dumps from loaders/dump_fluree.py. Regenerable in seconds, ~9 MB
# each, and a checked-in backup goes stale the moment the ledger moves —
# which is worse than no backup, because it looks like one.
backups/

# portable/dump/dump_all.sh's local staging area for workstation-side dump
# output (a live SQLite snapshot, a real Neo4j graph export) before it ships
# to db1. Never commit a database into this repo.
.b1-staging/
